# Contracts and deployment shape. A change here reaches every service.
/proto/                      @MyauDev/admin
/deploy/k8s/base/            @MyauDev/admin

# The RLS-exemption allowlist (openspec design D4, change 0.2). Adding a
# table here is a security decision: it says no tenant's data can ever land
# in it.
/deploy/db/rls-exempt-tables.txt   @MyauDev/admin

# The shared+tenant list (change 3.1 design D2a). It means the opposite of the
# file above: a table listed here holds tenant rows AND rows belonging to
# nobody. Listing one does not relax the coverage test -- it swaps one exact
# requirement for a stricter pair -- but deciding that a table has unowned rows
# at all is a security decision.
/deploy/db/rls-shared-tenant-tables.txt   @MyauDev/admin

# The transaction seam and the tenant identifier. db.InTx is the one place a
# tenant context is set, and db.OrgID is the type no other package can
# construct -- a change to either is a change to what "acting for a tenant"
# means (design D2, D7).
/core/internal/db/           @MyauDev/admin

# Money is int64 minor units plus an ISO-4217 code, never a float. The
# checked-in exponent table decides how every amount is scaled.
/core/internal/money/        @MyauDev/admin

# A worker takes its tenant identifier from its job arguments and from nowhere
# else. River's tables carry no org_id and no policy, so this package is the
# one place tenancy is not enforced by the database (design D5).
/core/internal/jobs/         @MyauDev/admin

# The tenancy migration: the four tenant tables, their policies, the
# NOLOGIN role vekst_membership_reader and the single SECURITY DEFINER
# function orgs_for_user. That function and that role are the only enumerated
# exceptions to "row-level security decides what is visible" (design D3), and
# a second of either is a security decision, not a refactor.
/core/migrations/00004_tenancy.sql   @MyauDev/admin

# The taxonomy migration: the first table that is shared and tenant at once,
# its split read/write policies, and the constraint trigger that stands in for
# a composite foreign key a shared parent cannot satisfy (change 3.1).
/core/migrations/00005_classification_taxonomy.sql   @MyauDev/admin

# The rules migration: template rules that belong to a country or a bank
# rather than to a customer, and the vendor memory that belongs to exactly one
# (change 3.2). The two tables sit side by side with opposite policies, which
# is the thing to read carefully in a review.
/core/migrations/00006_classification_rules.sql   @MyauDev/admin

# The canonical ledger migration (change 2.5): import_batches, transactions
# and classifications. The append-only enforcement on classifications is a
# deferred constraint trigger, not a plain unique index -- a plain one
# cannot express "insert the new live row, then point the old one at it"
# without a race, which was verified directly against this schema before
# being corrected. Read that trigger's own comment before touching either
# half of a supersede.
/core/migrations/00007_transaction_ledger.sql   @MyauDev/admin
/core/internal/db/query/ledger.sql              @MyauDev/admin
/core/internal/ledger/                          @MyauDev/admin

# D1-D5 and internal transfers (change 2.6). import_batches_file_once is
